<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
  <!--
    Entitlements for the TOP-LEVEL app bundle ONLY (macOS).

    Identical to entitlements.plist plus `keychain-access-groups`, and the split
    exists because that one key CANNOT be applied to every binary in the bundle.

    WHY, recorded because the failure is completely opaque: `optionsForFile` used
    to return one entitlements file for every path @electron/osx-sign visits,
    which includes nested helper binaries like ripgrep's `rg` and node-pty's
    `spawn-helper`. Signing `rg` with a keychain access group it is not
    provisioned for produces a binary that `codesign -v` calls "valid on disk"
    and that "satisfies its Designated Requirement" — and that the kernel then
    SIGKILLs on exec (exit 137) with no output, no stderr and nothing in the
    system log. The self-test caught it as `ripgrep: FAIL`.

    So: this file for the app bundle (codesign applies a bundle's entitlements to
    its main executable, which is the process that calls app.configureWebAuthn),
    and entitlements.plist for everything else, including the inherit case.

    The access group MUST stay byte-identical to the `keychainAccessGroup` passed
    in src/main/webauthn.ts — a mismatch fails at credential-creation time, not at
    launch. Shape is <TEAM_ID>.<CFBundleIdentifier>.webauthn; the team id is the
    one in forge.config.ts's signing identity, and the bundle id is
    `com.electron.atlarix`, @electron/packager's DEFAULT because packagerConfig
    sets no `appBundleId`. Renaming that bundle id is its own release — see
    docs/TODO.md — so this string changes only when that does.
  -->
  <dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables</key>
    <true/>
    <!-- Retain user-granted folder access across launches in signed builds so
         the macOS folder-access prompt isn't repeated. Inert on win/linux. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-write</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.files.bookmarks.app-scope</key>
    <true/>
    <!-- WebAuthn / passkeys (Electron >= 42, macOS only). Touch ID credentials
         are stored under this keychain access group. -->
    <key>keychain-access-groups</key>
    <array>
      <string>7VWW7N9R87.com.electron.atlarix.webauthn</string>
    </array>
  </dict>
</plist>
